WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.720
- [Male] Who turned you into this guy?

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:02.800
Did something turn you--

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:04.790
- America and my mother.

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:05.860
I was born in the Soviet Union.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:07.040
I didn't come up with much.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.450
So adversity, America, my mom and my dad

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:12.520
because when I was 14 and started working

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.270
at my dad's liquor store
for two bucks an hour,

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:15.730
I was so full of shit

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:18.030
and he sucked it out of me over 10 years

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:20.020
'cause he hates lying and bullshit.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:20.870
And so he scared me.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:23.070
I was scared to embellish, let alone lie.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.477
I don't get upset when people are like,

00:00:24.477 --> 00:00:25.572
"Yo, this guy's full of shit,"

00:00:25.572 --> 00:00:27.250
because I'm like, I understand.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:28.876
I actually had that in me.

00:00:28.876 --> 00:00:31.370
But my dad systematically took it out

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:34.160
and I think it comes
from lack of entitlement.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.220
I turned 14

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.290
and by being the oldest
son in an immigrant family,

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:40.040
my dad dragged me into the small store

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.080
in Springfield, New Jersey,
Shopper's Discount Liquors.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.101
I went from making $2,000 a weekend

00:00:45.101 --> 00:00:48.300
looking cool to the
ladies with all my cash

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.560
to being paid two bucks an hour to bag ice

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.750
in my dad's liquor store
for 10 hours a day.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:54.790
And more importantly,

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:56.400
I wasn't allowed to leave the basement.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.290
I was truly like Sloth from Goonies.

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:00.320
I was like, "Hey, you guys."

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:01.450
Terrible, hated it.

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:04.380
Used to cry going to work for
the first year and a half.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:05.782
Finally, when I was 16,

00:01:05.782 --> 00:01:08.150
my dad let me leave the basement.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:09.930
So I went upstairs to dust bottles

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:12.884
and in 1998, I took
over the family business

00:01:12.884 --> 00:01:14.900
and from 1998 to 2005,

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:16.713
I grew the revenue of Wine Library

00:01:16.713 --> 00:01:19.330
from a three to a $60
million a year business.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:22.840
I had a burning desire
to pay back my parents

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:23.897
for being the best.

00:01:23.897 --> 00:01:25.702
I was passionate,

00:01:25.702 --> 00:01:29.910
like just obsessed with building
my family business for them

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:33.410
because they had put me on
so much, emotionally, not,

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:34.460
people are always saying,

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:35.687
when people like to razz me, they're like,

00:01:35.687 --> 00:01:36.520
"Easy for you to say,

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:37.970
"you were given a liquor store."

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:39.360
I wasn't.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.644
I built a business for my family

00:01:41.644 --> 00:01:43.950
and left with no economics

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:47.695
because emotionally my
parents perfectly parented me

00:01:47.695 --> 00:01:50.470
which is now been the
foundation of all my success.

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:52.576
There's only one way to
win a family business,

00:01:52.576 --> 00:01:54.760
radical candor.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.670
And you know how easy that
is in a family business?

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:58.100
Almost impossible.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.290
So I'm giving you advice
that's hard, radical candor.

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:01.123
You're just gonna lose,

00:02:01.123 --> 00:02:02.261
it's gonna eat you inside.

00:02:02.261 --> 00:02:03.740
And I have good news for you.

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:04.650
This is the only chance

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.100
to have a good relationship with them,

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.350
not the other way around.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:09.500
You think you're doing the right thing

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:10.836
to have a good relationship with them.

00:02:10.836 --> 00:02:12.260
You're leading to the things

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.092
that's gonna make you have regrets

00:02:14.092 --> 00:02:16.290
and real issues with them.

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:17.220
Then you're gonna snap

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:18.850
and you guys are gonna
have a bad relationship.

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:21.093
Second, third generation
family business people

00:02:21.093 --> 00:02:23.360
pay the biggest tax in the world

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:24.793
and when they're fucking talented,

00:02:24.793 --> 00:02:26.220
they really are fucked.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.590
You know what family business
dynamics are good for?

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:30.563
Losers.

00:02:30.563 --> 00:02:32.800
That's when you're lucky.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:33.633
When you suck shit

00:02:33.633 --> 00:02:35.810
and you're the one benefiting.

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:37.400
You know when family businesses suck?

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.170
When you're a winner.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.770
And you're paying a tax
nobody's ever paid in life.

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:43.660
It wasn't about how
many impressions you got

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:45.840
or how many awards you
won in fucking Cannes.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.000
It was about if we didn't
sell fucking pinot noir,

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.208
we weren't gonna eat.

00:02:49.208 --> 00:02:51.740
So it was results driven.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:52.850
My dad and I,

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:54.240
for all the things we agreed on,

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.670
all the things we disagreed on,

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:56.950
thank God we loved each other

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:58.740
just enough more than
we loved the business

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:00.800
which allowed us to keep a relationship.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.036
But 99% of our energy was
always about the business

00:03:04.036 --> 00:03:05.281
for the long term.

00:03:05.281 --> 00:03:07.527
It's easy for everybody
from the outside saying,

00:03:07.527 --> 00:03:09.607
"Yo bro, stop carrying
your sister and your uncle

00:03:09.607 --> 00:03:11.404
"and everybody's getting
money off your back."

00:03:11.404 --> 00:03:13.620
If it makes you feel good and you're okay

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.470
with 15 cents on the dollar
or 50 cents on the dollar,

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:17.430
well then that's your life.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:18.799
So I would say this,

00:03:18.799 --> 00:03:20.800
if you've got a problem with it

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.690
and your friends are saying that

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:23.720
because they're supporting

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:25.730
what you're saying after four beers,

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:26.563
that's one thing.

00:03:26.563 --> 00:03:28.265
But if they're just
observing from the outside

00:03:28.265 --> 00:03:30.175
and they're starting to change your mind,

00:03:30.175 --> 00:03:32.294
I wouldn't let outside sources

00:03:32.294 --> 00:03:35.390
tell you what to do with
inside of family dynamic.

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:36.492
At the end of the day,

00:03:36.492 --> 00:03:41.040
if you are the parent in the
situation of a family business

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:42.295
or if you are the child

00:03:42.295 --> 00:03:44.447
in the situation of a family business,

00:03:44.447 --> 00:03:46.365
you have to deeply understand

00:03:46.365 --> 00:03:49.118
that when both of you
are done and finished,

00:03:49.118 --> 00:03:50.660
nobody's gonna count

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:52.870
how many zeroes they
have in the bank account,

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:56.516
they're gonna count how many
hearts and vibes and loves

00:03:56.516 --> 00:03:57.822
are in the system.

00:03:57.822 --> 00:04:01.350
What my dad and my brother
and I were capable of doing

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:04.417
was loving each other
just a little bit more

00:04:04.417 --> 00:04:07.210
than the business making some more money

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:10.264
or the ego or insecurity
scratches that we needed

00:04:10.264 --> 00:04:12.876
in the dynamic of our relationships.

00:04:12.876 --> 00:04:16.411
If there's anything that this
film could get across to you

00:04:16.411 --> 00:04:19.920
I hope it is that at the end
of the day on that last breath

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.720
ask yourself what you're gonna
be really thinking about.

